# Files that run on a Unix shell, or that a Unix tool parses, must keep LF line endings, even in a
# checkout made by Windows git with core.autocrlf=true (for WSL, on /mnt/c): `#!/bin/sh\r` fails
# with "bad interpreter", before any Python can run and say what is wrong, and `.env.example` or a
# Makefile with CRLF breaks Make and the dotenv parsers.
platform/platformctl text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf
Makefile text eol=lf
*/Makefile text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
*/Dockerfile text eol=lf
Dockerfile.* text eol=lf
*.Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
.env.example text eol=lf
*/.env.example text eol=lf

# NOTICE.txt is committed with CRLF and a byte-order mark; leave it exactly as it is.
NOTICE.txt -text
